        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Raquel A Villamizar work for?\n\nAnswer A: University of Pamplona\n  about: Raquel A Villamizar\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Raquel A Villamizar has worked at the University of Pamplona.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: the university\n  about: Raquel A Villamizar\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"I've just joined the university as a researcher in the biology department,\" she introduced herself,\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Raquel A Villamizar\n\nRaquel A Villamizar is a researcher with the ORCID identifier 0000-0001-5225-0579. Career\n\nRaquel A Villamizar has worked at the University of Pamplona. Evidence\n\nAccording to her ORCID profile, Raquel A Villamizar has listed the University of Pamplona as one of her affiliations. [1] This suggests that she has worked or is currently working at the university. Additionally, a search on the University of Pamplona's website reveals that Raquel A Villamizar has been involved in research projects and has published papers in collaboration with other researchers from the university. [2]\n\nFurthermore, her ResearchGate profile, a social networking site for scientists and researchers, also lists the University of Pamplona as one of her affiliations, providing further evidence of her association with the university. [3]\n\nReferences\n\n[1] ORCID. (n.d.). Raquel A Villamizar. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/orcid.org\\/0000-0001-5225-0579>\n\n[2] University of Pamplona. (n.d.). Research Projects. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.unipamplona.edu.co\\/investigacion\\/proyectos-de-investigacion\\/>\n\n[3] ResearchGate. (n.d.). Raquel A Villamizar.\nDocument 2: As Dr. Sophia Patel sipped her lukewarm coffee, she gazed out the rain-streaked window of her office at the University of Manchester. The grey Manchester morning matched her mood, still reeling from the previous day's meeting with the department head. Her research on genetic mutations was on the cusp of a breakthrough, but the funding was precarious at best. Just as she was about to refocus on her notes, a knock at the door broke the silence. \"Come in,\" Sophia called out, expecting one of her students or a colleague. Instead, a woman with curly dark hair and a warm smile entered, carrying a worn leather satchel. \"Dr. Patel, I'm Raquel Villamizar. I've just joined the university as a researcher in the biology department,\" she introduced herself, her accent a subtle blend of Latin American rhythms and British precision. Sophia's curiosity was piqued. \"Welcome to Manchester, Raquel. What brings you to our humble institution?\" Raquel's eyes sparkled as she began to unpack her satchel, revealing a collection of dog-eared journals and a sleek laptop. \"I'm excited to collaborate with the team here. My background is in microbiology, and I've been following your work on genetic mutations. I think we could make some fascinating discoveries together.\" As they delved into a discussion about the intricacies of gene expression, Sophia found herself drawn to Raquel's infectious enthusiasm and impressive credentials. It wasn't until they exchanged contact information that Sophia noticed the ORCID identifier on Raquel's business card: 0000-0001-5225-0579. A quick mental note was made to look up Raquel's publications later, but for now, Sophia was content to bask in the promise of a new partnership. As Raquel departed, leaving behind a trail of freshly sharpened pencils and a hint of citrus perfume, Sophia felt a glimmer of hope. Perhaps this chance encounter would be the catalyst her research needed.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
